From b69bac78bccaa1c9199ca95fc148db42fecaa710 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivan Malison <IvanMalison@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 01:40:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fix Objects panel row drag crash

Dragging a nested object or group row in the Objects dialog can abort inside GTK before Inkscape's own drop handler gets control. The crash comes from GTK's native TreeView row-reordering path: when the Objects panel enables native row reordering over the name column, GTK requests the built-in GTK_TREE_MODEL_ROW target and serializes the nested GtkTreeStore path through gtk_tree_set_row_drag_data(). With a deep path such as 0:0:0, that GTK 3 serialization path can trip fortified bounds checking and abort with a buffer-overflow failure.

The Objects panel already implements its own drag/drop behavior in on_drag_drop(), so it does not need GTK's built-in row-reordering target. Register an Inkscape-owned same-widget DND target for both the source and destination, provide inert selection data for that target, and keep native TreeView reordering disabled even when the pointer is over the name column. This preserves the existing panel reparenting behavior while avoiding the GTK row serialization path that aborts.

Add a headless regression test that models the actual crash path instead of only checking the configured target list. The test constructs a nested Gtk::TreeStore row, prepares GTK_TREE_MODEL_ROW selection data in a subprocess, and would request GTK's native row drag data if the Objects panel policy ever re-enables native tree reordering over the name column. With the fix, the subprocess exits normally without touching that GTK crash path. When forced down the old native-row path locally, the same test fails with signal 6 and "*** buffer overflow detected ***", matching the original failure mode.

The helper target construction functions are kept file-local; the test only depends on the policy hook that decides whether Objects panel drags should use native TreeView row reordering.

The interactive Xvfb repro was also run manually: the baseline crashed while the patched build survived the same nested-row drag and moved the object as expected. A supplemental branch with the coordinate-driven Xvfb/xdotool repro harness is available at https://gitlab.com/ivanmalison/inkscape-fork/-/tree/colonelpanic/objects-panel-dnd-crash-5256-with-repro. It is intentionally not part of this patch because it depends on local GUI geometry and tooling rather than Inkscape's normal test harness.

Verified with:

- test_objects-panel rebuilt and run directly
- forced old-path test variant failed with signal 6 and "*** buffer overflow detected ***"
- ctest --test-dir build-debug --output-on-failure -R '^test_objects-panel$'
- ctest -R 'test_(drag-and-drop-svgz|sp-item|sp-object|object-set|sp-item-group|objects-panel)$'
- automated Xvfb nested-row drag repro against the patched build

https://gitlab.com/inkscape/inkscape/-/work_items/5256
